# All variables are required
#
# This file is the development template. Tests don't read it or a copy of it:
# `.env.test` holds DATABASE_URL and nothing else, and everything else the suite
# needs is pinned by src/server/test-utils/test-env.ts.

DATABASE_URL=postgresql://
CRYPTO_PEPPER=something-random
PORT=3000
APP_NAME=Billet CMS
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Optional — the canonical origin used for <link rel="canonical">, Open Graph
# tags, the sitemap, robots.txt's Sitemap: line, and JSON-LD. Defaults to
# APP_URL's origin, which is right for almost every deployment: keeping the
# canonical domain equal to the domain the CSRF check compares the request
# against is the point. Set it only when the two genuinely differ — a marketing site on the
# apex with the app on a subdomain (SITE_URL=https://example.com while
# APP_URL=https://app.example.com), or a canonical on www when the app is not.
# Unset or blank means the origin follows APP_URL. Any other value must be an
# absolute http(s) URL — anything else is fatal at boot.
# SITE_URL=https://example.com

# Optional — let search engines index the site. Unset (or anything other than
# `true`) keeps the whole site out of Google: every response carries
# `X-Robots-Tag: noindex, nofollow`, every page renders
# `<meta name="robots" content="noindex, nofollow">`, and robots.txt drops its
# Sitemap line. Set it on the production host, and only there — a preview or
# staging environment that never sets it cannot be indexed by accident.
# Crawling stays allowed on purpose: a crawler has to fetch a page to be told
# noindex. See runbooks/SEO.md §1b.
# ALLOW_INDEXING=true

# Optional — defaults to "cms_session". Set a project-unique value when running
# multiple billet apps on localhost so their cookies don't collide across ports.
# Cookies are not scoped by port, so without this a sign-in on :3001 overwrites
# the session on :3002. In a Conductor workspace, DATABASE_URL, PORT, APP_URL
# and this are all rewritten per-workspace by `scripts/workspace.ts provision`.
# SESSION_COOKIE_NAME=myapp_session

# The site's own Billet app, where CMS sign-in happens. Required: the CMS has
# no sign-in of its own, so a signed-out visit is redirected to
# <SITE_APP_URL>/cms/sign-in, a route the site mounts from the plugin, which
# mints a single-use handoff token and sends the browser back. Without it the
# CMS can sign nobody in at all, so the server refuses to boot. Set it to the
# site's origin, e.g. https://example.com.
SITE_APP_URL=https://example.com

# Optional — Contact for /.well-known/security.txt. A bare email is wrapped in
# mailto:; a mailto:/https:/tel: URI is used as-is. Defaults to a placeholder
# (security@<the canonical site host>). Set a monitored address before launch.
# See runbooks/SECURITY.md §2.
# SECURITY_CONTACT=security@yourdomain.com

# Optional — set to "true" to serve a 503 "under maintenance" page (with a
# Retry-After header) for every request except the /health check. An app-level
# fallback; prefer flipping maintenance at your edge/CDN when you can, since the
# app is what you're taking offline. MAINTENANCE_RETRY_AFTER overrides the
# Retry-After value in seconds (default 3600).
# MAINTENANCE_MODE=true
# MAINTENANCE_RETRY_AFTER=3600

# Optional — set to "true" when the app runs behind exactly one reverse proxy that
# rewrites or appends to x-forwarded-for itself (Railway does). The rate limiter
# then keys on the last entry of that header — the hop the proxy added — instead of
# the socket address, which behind a proxy is the proxy. Off by default: without a
# proxy in front, the header is client-controlled and trusting it lets anyone walk
# past the limiter with a fresh value per request. Any value other than
# "true"/"false" stops the server at boot.
# TRUST_PROXY=true

# Optional — the S3-compatible bucket the CMS stores media in. The bucket name,
# both API credentials and the public base URL are required together: uploads
# are refused with a message until all four are set, because storing bytes the
# site cannot then load is worse than failing the upload. MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL
# is the origin stored renditions are served from (it must be an absolute
# http(s) URL; it also joins the CSP `img-src`). S3_REGION and S3_ENDPOINT are
# for providers that need them (an AWS deployment needs neither);
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN is for temporary credentials.
# S3_BUCKET=my-bucket
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=...
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=...
# MEDIA_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=https://media.example.com
# S3_REGION=eu-west-2
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com
# S3_SESSION_TOKEN=...
